1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,184
A coach draws one straight chalk
line across the college ground.

2
00:00:04,584 --> 00:00:08,996
Now every spot on the ground is on
one side, on the other side, or right

3
00:00:08,996 --> 00:00:09,941
on the line.

4
00:00:10,391 --> 00:00:14,079
Aarav is standing at one spot,
and wonders which side he is on.

5
00:00:14,429 --> 00:00:16,963
One line splits the whole field in two.

6
00:00:16,963 --> 00:00:19,591
So how does maths decide which side wins?

7
00:00:20,091 --> 00:00:23,169
The answer is an inequality
in two variables.

8
00:00:23,169 --> 00:00:25,783
And its answer is not a few numbers.

9
00:00:25,783 --> 00:00:27,436
It is a whole region.

10
00:00:28,086 --> 00:00:30,955
First, think of the ground as graph paper.

11
00:00:30,955 --> 00:00:32,816
Every spot has two numbers.

12
00:00:32,816 --> 00:00:36,106
x tells how far across,
and y tells how far up.

13
00:00:36,506 --> 00:00:40,412
An equation like x plus y
equals six is a straight line.

14
00:00:40,412 --> 00:00:43,355
Every point on it adds up to exactly six.

15
00:00:43,755 --> 00:00:44,735
Zero and six.

16
00:00:44,735 --> 00:00:45,715
Two and four.

17
00:00:45,715 --> 00:00:46,695
Six and zero.

18
00:00:46,695 --> 00:00:47,866
All on the line.

19
00:00:48,466 --> 00:00:50,649
Now change equals to less than.

20
00:00:50,649 --> 00:00:52,640
x plus y is less than six.

21
00:00:53,040 --> 00:00:57,016
The points that add up to less than
six are not on the line any more.

22
00:00:57,016 --> 00:00:58,962
They all sit on one side of it.

23
00:00:59,612 --> 00:01:01,122
Watch the test run.

24
00:01:01,122 --> 00:01:04,152
We drop points on the plane, one by one.

25
00:01:04,552 --> 00:01:05,452
One and one.

26
00:01:05,443 --> 00:01:06,828
Two is less than six.

27
00:01:06,828 --> 00:01:07,728
True.

28
00:01:07,312 --> 00:01:08,688
The point turns green.

29
00:01:09,088 --> 00:01:10,151
Five and four.

30
00:01:10,151 --> 00:01:11,948
Nine is not less than six.

31
00:01:11,948 --> 00:01:12,848
False.

32
00:01:12,522 --> 00:01:13,422
Red.

33
00:01:13,402 --> 00:01:14,302
Keep going.

34
00:01:14,254 --> 00:01:16,681
Every green point lands below the line.

35
00:01:16,681 --> 00:01:18,681
Every red point lands above it.

36
00:01:19,282 --> 00:01:23,260
So the line cuts the plane into
two halves, called half planes.

37
00:01:23,260 --> 00:01:25,984
One half is the answer, and we shade it.

38
00:01:26,634 --> 00:01:28,199
Here is the shortcut.

39
00:01:28,199 --> 00:01:31,299
Pick one test point
that is not on the line.

40
00:01:31,299 --> 00:01:34,227
The easiest is the origin, zero and zero.

41
00:01:34,627 --> 00:01:36,334
Zero plus zero is zero.

42
00:01:36,334 --> 00:01:37,991
Zero is less than six.

43
00:01:37,991 --> 00:01:38,891
True.

44
00:01:38,553 --> 00:01:41,295
So shade the side that holds the origin.

45
00:01:41,895 --> 00:01:45,691
If the test comes out false,
shade the other side instead.

46
00:01:46,341 --> 00:01:47,357
Side by side.

47
00:01:47,357 --> 00:01:50,753
With less than, the points
on the line do not count.

48
00:01:50,753 --> 00:01:52,597
So we draw the line dashed.

49
00:01:52,997 --> 00:01:56,394
With less than or equal
to, the line counts too.

50
00:01:56,394 --> 00:01:57,966
So we draw it solid.

51
00:01:58,566 --> 00:01:59,466
Your turn.

52
00:01:59,405 --> 00:02:01,997
Two x plus three y is at least twelve.

53
00:02:01,997 --> 00:02:03,182
Test the origin.

54
00:02:03,182 --> 00:02:05,958
Shade the origin side, or the other side?

55
00:02:05,958 --> 00:02:07,191
Pause and decide.

56
00:02:10,391 --> 00:02:11,568
The other side!

57
00:02:11,568 --> 00:02:15,270
Zero is not at least twelve,
so the origin side fails.

58
00:02:15,270 --> 00:02:18,552
And the line is solid,
because equal is allowed.

59
00:02:19,202 --> 00:02:20,175
Remember this.

60
00:02:20,175 --> 00:02:22,104
A line cuts the plane in two.

61
00:02:22,104 --> 00:02:24,632
One test point tells you which half wins.

62
00:02:25,333 --> 00:02:27,390
Some inequalities have only one letter.

63
00:02:27,390 --> 00:02:28,965
Take x is greater than two.

64
00:02:29,365 --> 00:02:31,578
On a number line, that was a ray.

65
00:02:31,578 --> 00:02:35,872
On the plane, it is every point to
the right of the line x equals two.

66
00:02:35,872 --> 00:02:37,157
A whole half plane.

67
00:02:37,757 --> 00:02:38,657
Quick one.

68
00:02:38,560 --> 00:02:40,038
y is less than three.

69
00:02:40,038 --> 00:02:43,177
Shade above the line y
equals three, or below it?

70
00:02:43,177 --> 00:02:44,313
Pause and think.

71
00:02:47,513 --> 00:02:48,413
Below!

72
00:02:48,069 --> 00:02:51,581
Every point lower than height
three works, whatever its x.

73
00:02:52,231 --> 00:02:53,921
Now, one worked example.

74
00:02:54,271 --> 00:02:58,261
Shade the region where three x
plus two y is greater than six.

75
00:02:58,761 --> 00:02:59,661
Step one.

76
00:02:59,583 --> 00:03:02,643
Draw the line three x
plus two y equals six.

77
00:03:02,643 --> 00:03:06,385
It meets the x axis at two,
and the y axis at three.

78
00:03:07,135 --> 00:03:08,035
Step two.

79
00:03:07,875 --> 00:03:10,473
The sign is greater than, with no equals.

80
00:03:10,473 --> 00:03:11,962
So the line is dashed.

81
00:03:12,712 --> 00:03:13,678
Step three.

82
00:03:13,678 --> 00:03:14,970
Test the origin.

83
00:03:14,970 --> 00:03:17,134
Zero is not greater than six.

84
00:03:17,134 --> 00:03:18,034
False.

85
00:03:18,525 --> 00:03:19,425
Step four.

86
00:03:19,307 --> 00:03:21,715
So shade the side away from the origin.

87
00:03:21,715 --> 00:03:23,611
That half plane is the answer.

88
00:03:24,261 --> 00:03:25,723
Now, the exam trap.

89
00:03:26,073 --> 00:03:28,391
Two x minus y is greater than four.

90
00:03:28,391 --> 00:03:31,942
Many students see greater
than, and shade above the line.

91
00:03:32,342 --> 00:03:33,883
But test the origin.

92
00:03:33,883 --> 00:03:36,064
Zero is not greater than four.

93
00:03:36,064 --> 00:03:36,964
False.

94
00:03:36,695 --> 00:03:40,748
The origin is above this line,
so the answer is below it.

95
00:03:40,748 --> 00:03:42,288
Always test a point.

96
00:03:42,288 --> 00:03:44,356
Never trust the symbol alone.

97
00:03:45,006 --> 00:03:46,418
Where is this asked?

98
00:03:46,418 --> 00:03:51,101
This part was removed from the current
NCERT text, so no board chip lights.

99
00:03:51,101 --> 00:03:55,849
The same test, which side of a line a
point is on, still helps in coordinate

100
00:03:55,849 --> 00:03:57,053
geometry problems.

101
00:03:57,653 --> 00:03:58,814
Exam level now.

102
00:03:58,814 --> 00:04:03,153
How many points with positive whole
number coordinates make this true?

103
00:04:03,153 --> 00:04:04,364
Pause and solve.

104
00:04:09,764 --> 00:04:11,350
The answer is B.

105
00:04:11,350 --> 00:04:12,250
Nine.

106
00:04:12,539 --> 00:04:14,564
Take each height y in turn.

107
00:04:14,564 --> 00:04:17,240
When y is one, x is less than six.

108
00:04:17,240 --> 00:04:18,904
So x is one to five.

109
00:04:18,904 --> 00:04:19,907
Five points.

110
00:04:20,407 --> 00:04:23,112
When y is two, x is less than four.

111
00:04:23,112 --> 00:04:24,158
Three points.

112
00:04:24,158 --> 00:04:26,914
When y is three, x is less than two.

113
00:04:26,914 --> 00:04:27,814
Just one.

114
00:04:28,255 --> 00:04:31,373
When y is four, x would
have to be less than zero.

115
00:04:31,373 --> 00:04:32,273
None.

116
00:04:31,862 --> 00:04:34,203
So five plus three plus one makes nine.

117
00:04:34,803 --> 00:04:36,343
Here is your recap.

118
00:04:36,343 --> 00:04:37,926
Screenshot this card.

119
00:04:41,126 --> 00:04:43,312
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:04:43,712 --> 00:04:44,612
One.

121
00:04:44,103 --> 00:04:45,879
What does a dashed line tell you?

122
00:04:46,779 --> 00:04:47,679
Two.

123
00:04:47,221 --> 00:04:49,545
Which test point is the easiest to use?

124
00:04:50,445 --> 00:04:51,345
Three.

125
00:04:51,020 --> 00:04:55,699
For y is greater than x, which side
of the line y equals x do you shade?

126
00:04:56,799 --> 00:04:59,161
Next time: three shirts and four pants.

127
00:04:59,161 --> 00:05:00,993
How many outfits can you make?

128
00:05:00,993 --> 00:05:04,458
We start counting, with the
fundamental principle of counting.
